592186045417] [17592186277928 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277928 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186275787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186277928 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "723b6093-df88-5ee2-b562-a30395cdbb69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278108 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186276501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278108 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186276519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278108 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186276903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278108 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186277157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278108 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186277175 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278108 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278108 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278108 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186276501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278108 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d99b0f37-11a6-51ae-b457-b0354976d304" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278424 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278424 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161207" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278424 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278424 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/4c/4cb2c8128f24adddacd82b5f477848faa47ae52f95a5bb0636a8b286a6628a08.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,{z}^{2}\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $2$ whose base is a circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278424 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f41018fb-b7c4-59de-93b6-b0fee9601c2c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278424 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,{z}^{2}\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/e6/e6fe01196298c1da0961fd01c73ee4cb7c71b6a03a027cceaba0e68be944c968.png)\n\n \nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=2$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{z}^{2}\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{2}^{2}\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}4\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =4{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,y)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =4{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}r\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}r\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}r\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}r\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=16-{r}^{2},$ we get $\\text{d}u=-2r\\,\\text{d}r.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & 0 & 2 \\\\ u & 16 & 12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}r\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r & =8\\pi \\cdot {\\int }_{16}^{12}\\sqrt{u}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{12}^{16}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4\\pi \\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{[{u}^{3/2}]}_{12}^{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}[64-24\\sqrt{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{64\\pi (8-3\\sqrt{3})}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,y,0)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,{0}^{2}\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{64\\pi (8-3\\sqrt{3})}{3}+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{64\\pi (8-3\\sqrt{3})}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278431 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278431 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161219" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278431 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278431 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/9a/9afc7f79f4612ce579e82a6b3c5086922d14051978907bfeb5c133a3f99791c7.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,x{y}^{2}+z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278431 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "df8892a4-0ff3-5c81-818d-9a1c55b02265" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278431 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,x{y}^{2}+z⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/fa/fa4986b1de44fa4ccc75114e64abfd93d0de16618f2f1b29695d9115019b6e3e.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=4$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=4$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,x{y}^{2}+z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,x{y}^{2}+4⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =x{y}^{2}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}x{y}^{2}+4\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}+4\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x{y}^{2}+4\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4x]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{y}^{2}+8\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}+8y]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,x{y}^{2}+z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,x{y}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-x{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}+(-\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278432 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278108 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278432 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161222" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278432 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278432 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/01/01902836fd1e91ac829f67a279103a9b3d5732d7b058f03c695b6733a42e673d.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278432 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8c3f4c19-765d-5833-904a-bf7538da3049" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278432 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+z⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/c0/c058465d6ca7855446711ebc44f6b23913ee786d2fd9bb32ae6c671964e1ad32.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=1$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=1$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+1⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+x{y}^{2}+x]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}+\\frac{5}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}+\\frac{5}{4}y]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-({x}^{3}+{y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-({x}^{3}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+x{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}+\\frac{1}{4}y]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{12}+(-\\frac{7}{12}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
